<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adelaide of Susa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1014–<a href="/years/1091">1091</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide-countess-of-vermandois">Adelaide</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a> was the heiress of the March of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> and one of the most powerful rulers in 11th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Her marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odo-count-of-savoy">Otto of Savoy</a> brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arduinici">Arduinici</a> lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> to the house of Savoy. After Otto's death she governed the march herself for decades, and her daughter Bertha married the emperor Henry IV.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adelaide-of-susa.jpg" alt="Adelaide of Susa" width="320" height="529" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adelaide_de_Suse.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
